-- Repair an inconsistency in an existing (authored) profile with the smallest edit. Binds $model (the rule model the
-- profile is consistent with), $profile ({APPLICABILITY, DECISION BOUNDARY, MET, NOT MET, UNCERTAIN}) and $finding (a
-- consistency check's report: {overlap, overlap_case, gap, gap_case}). The rule model decides where the case belongs — MET
-- holds when the behaviour occurs, every condition holds and no exception applies; NOT MET is its complement; UNCERTAIN only
-- names facts the rule leaves open — and only MET, NOT MET and UNCERTAIN may change, by the fewest words that place the case
-- in exactly one field. Replies with the three fields (unchanged when no edit is needed); tidy them with $tidy.
$json%($parse%(=>("A consistency check found a problem in an evaluation profile its authors wrote. Repair it with the smallest edit: change only the words needed so the CASE below falls in exactly one of MET and NOT MET — where the RULE MODEL puts it (MET holds when the behaviour occurs, every condition holds and no exception applies; NOT MET is the complement) — or in UNCERTAIN only if it turns on a fact the model says can be unclear. Keep every other word, the authors' style and their order; do not restate the model or add explanation; leave a field exactly as written if it needs no change.\n\nReply with JSON only: {\"MET\": \"<the field>\", \"NOT MET\": \"<the field>\", \"UNCERTAIN\": \"<the field>\", \"why\": \"<one sentence: where the case belongs and why>\"}\n\nFINDING:\n" ++ $json%$finding ++ "\n\nRULE MODEL:\n" ++ $json%$model ++ "\n\nPROFILE:\n" ++ $json%$profile)))
